# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# EmailJS — powers the contact form via app/api/contact/route.ts
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The form used to send directly from the browser via EmailJS. It now POSTs
# to our own /api/contact Route Handler, which enforces the per-IP rate
# limit below and only then calls EmailJS's REST API server-side using the
# Private Key — so these are plain secrets now, NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_* (a client
# that skipped the rate limit by calling EmailJS directly with just the
# Public Key can no longer send at all: EmailJS requires the Private Key
# `accessToken` for non-browser requests, see step 5).
#
# Setup:
#   1. Create a free account: https://dashboard.emailjs.com/sign-up
#   2. Email Services → Add New Service (e.g. Gmail) → copy its Service ID
#      → EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID
#   3. Email Templates → Create New Template. Set "To Email" to
#      luiszrita@gmail.com and use these variables (they must match
#      app/api/contact/route.ts exactly, or fields silently render blank):
#        {{email}}        — visitor's address; set this as the template's
#                           "Reply To" field so replying goes to them (the
#                           "From" address itself stays your EmailJS-connected
#                           account — providers block spoofing an arbitrary
#                           "From" over your own SMTP/Gmail API)
#        {{name}}         — no separate "name" field exists, so this is the
#                           visitor's email again; used for "From Name" and
#                           anywhere the template shows a display name
#        {{title}}        — fixed string sent by the form, for the subject
#        {{time}}         — submission timestamp
#        {{message}}      — the message body
#        {{to_email}}     — luiszrita@gmail.com (sent for convenience; only
#                           takes effect if "To Email" is set to this
#                           variable instead of typed in as plain text)
#      → copy the Template ID → EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID
#   4. Account → General → copy your Public Key → EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY
#   5. Account → Security:
#      - Copy your Private Key → EMAILJS_PRIVATE_KEY
#      - Enable "Allow EmailJS API requests from non-browser applications"
#        — required for server-side sends (browser origin checks don't
#        apply to our Route Handler, so this must be on, but every request
#        must now include the Private Key, which only our server holds).
#      - Optionally still restrict allowed origins to your domain, as
#        defense in depth.
#      EmailJS's free tier is capped at 200 emails/month.
#
# EMAILJS_SERVICE_ID=
# EMAILJS_TEMPLATE_ID=
# EMAILJS_PUBLIC_KEY=
# EMAILJS_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Upstash Redis — backs the contact form's rate limit (lib/rate-limit.ts)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Limits contact form submissions to 2 per IP per day. Uses Upstash's REST
# API (no persistent connections), which is what makes the count correct
# across Vercel's serverless instances — an in-memory counter would not be,
# since each request can land on a different instance.
#
# Setup:
#   1. https://console.upstash.com/ → create a free Redis database (pick a
#      region close to where the app is deployed).
#   2. Database → REST API — copy the two values below.
#   3. If deploying on Vercel: Upstash also has a Vercel integration that
#      sets both variables automatically for all environments.
#
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
# UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Google Analytics 4 — page + custom event tracking (lib/analytics.ts,
# app/layout.tsx via @next/third-parties/google)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* because the gtag.js script that reads this ID runs in the
# browser — it's a public measurement ID, not a secret.
#
# The GoogleAnalytics component only mounts when NODE_ENV=production AND
# this variable is set (see app/layout.tsx), so `next dev` never sends
# hits to your real GA4 property. To test locally, run a production build
# instead: `npm run build && npm run start`.
#
# Setup:
#   1. https://analytics.google.com/ → Admin → Create Property (if you
#      don't have one yet for this site).
#   2. In the new property: Data Streams → Add stream → Web → enter the
#      site URL (https://luisccz.com) → Create stream.
#   3. Copy the "Measurement ID" shown (format G-XXXXXXXXXX) → paste below.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in the values above.
# .env.local is already git-ignored — never commit real keys.
